# Instructor graph review mode — design

**Status:** implemented and verified, against both store backends. §2's gating was revised after the first implementation — a deployment-wide `GRAPH_EDIT_MODE` env var became a per-upload checkbox — see that section for why. Deviations found while building are in §11.
**Scope:** adds an opt-in "review mode" that pauses pipeline 1 after `graph_builder.build_graph()` so a human can edit the extracted concept graph (add/edit/delete concepts, add/delete prerequisite edges) before `question_generator.generate_questions()` runs. Off by default — an upload that doesn't ask for review is byte-for-byte what it is today. Touches `backend/app/models/schemas.py`, `backend/app/services/graph_builder.py`, `backend/app/store/memory_store.py`, `backend/app/store/postgres_store.py`, `backend/app/db/models.py` (+ one Alembic migration), `backend/app/routers/ingestion.py`, a new `backend/app/routers/graph_edit.py`, `backend/app/routers/study_session.py`, `backend/app/main.py`, and on the frontend `frontend/src/types/index.ts`, `frontend/src/api/client.ts`, a new `frontend/src/pages/ReviewGraphPage.tsx`, `frontend/src/App.tsx`, `frontend/src/pages/MenuPage.tsx`, `frontend/src/components/DocumentList.tsx`, `frontend/src/index.css`.

## 1. Context

`graph_builder.py`'s own regression suite (`tests/graph_geval`, see CLAUDE.md) is honest about where the extractor still falls short: edge recall is weak (backbone prerequisite edges get missed even when both endpoint concepts are extracted correctly), some quiz-worthy sub-concepts get folded away, and a couple of forbidden ideas leak through as their own nodes. None of that is a bug to fix in the prompt alone — it's the ceiling of "infer chapter structure from raw text with no human in the loop." The natural next step isn't a better prompt, it's a review step: let a person look at the graph the LLM produced and fix it before the (expensive, per-concept) question-generation pass runs against it.

This has to be opt-in. Most uploads should keep going straight through — review is for someone curating a chapter they intend to reuse across many students, not for casual use. The mode gates the *entire* review workflow behind one flag; nothing about default behavior changes when it's off.

## 2. Gating: `review` on the upload

**Revised after the first implementation — see the note at the end of this section for what this replaced and why.**

Review is a per-upload choice, expressed as one field on the existing upload payload (`backend/app/routers/ingestion.py`):

```python
class TextbookUpload(BaseModel):
    text: str = Field(min_length=1, description="Raw chapter text")
    title: str | None = None
    review: bool = Field(
        default=False,
        description="Stop after graph extraction so the concept graph can be edited, "
        "instead of generating questions immediately. The chapter stays a DRAFT until "
        "POST /api/textbook/{doc_id}/finalize.",
    )
```

Default `False`, so a client that has never heard of the field gets exactly the pipeline it always got. On the frontend it's a checkbox under the upload form, unticked by default (§7).

Because the choice is recorded as the chapter's own `DocumentStatus`, **draft status is then the entire gate** on everything downstream. The editing endpoints don't consult any setting; they ask whether *this chapter* is a draft. A chapter uploaded without `review` is `FINALIZED` from the moment it exists, so it's refused by the editing endpoints on exactly the same terms as a chapter whose review was already approved — same 409, same message. There is no second switch to keep in sync with the first, and no state where the feature is "on" but a given chapter isn't editable for a different reason.

**What this replaced.** This shipped first as `GRAPH_EDIT_MODE`, a deployment-wide env var on `Settings` alongside `database_url`, with every mutating endpoint 404ing when it was off and a `GET /api/config` for the frontend to discover it. The reasoning was that a per-upload flag "would let any caller silently skip question generation on any deployment, including ones the operator never intended to allow that on." That argument doesn't survive contact with this app: there is no auth, so the caller *is* the operator, and the thing being protected — spending fewer LLM calls, then being asked to approve a graph — isn't a privilege. What the env var actually bought was a feature nobody could reach without editing `.env` and restarting the backend, for a decision that belongs to whoever is uploading a specific chapter. Removing it deleted a setting, an endpoint, a 404 branch in two routers, and a test fixture that had to patch `lru_cache`d settings per router module.

## 3. Data model changes

New enum, next to `StudySessionStatus` in `backend/app/models/schemas.py`:

```python
class DocumentStatus(str, Enum):
    DRAFT = "draft"
    FINALIZED = "finalized"
```

A document is `DRAFT` from the moment an upload asks for `review` and its graph has been extracted but not yet approved; `FINALIZED` once questions exist and it's usable for study sessions. An upload that doesn't ask for review goes straight to `FINALIZED`, same as today — nothing is ever observably `DRAFT` unless someone chose it for that chapter.

`Store` ABC changes (`backend/app/store/memory_store.py`):

```python
@abstractmethod
def save_document(
    self, doc_id: str, text: str, title: str | None = None,
    status: DocumentStatus = DocumentStatus.FINALIZED,
) -> None: ...

@abstractmethod
def get_document_status(self, doc_id: str) -> DocumentStatus | None: ...

@abstractmethod
def finalize_document(self, doc_id: str) -> None:
    """Flip a document to FINALIZED. No-op if already finalized or unknown."""
    ...

@abstractmethod
def delete_concept(self, concept_id: str) -> None:
    """Remove a single concept row — draft-mode graph editing only; normal ingestion
    never deletes a concept after the fact. Idempotent. Does not clean up references
    to this id in other concepts' depends_on/evidence; callers load the whole graph
    anyway (to build the edited version), so they update those concepts themselves
    and persist them via save_graph()."""
    ...
```

`save_document`'s new `status` kwarg defaults to `FINALIZED`, so every existing call site (only `routers/ingestion.py`, plus ~28 direct calls across `test_memory_store.py`/`test_postgres_store.py`, all positional and none passing a 4th arg) keeps working unchanged.

**`InMemoryStore`:**
```python
def __init__(self) -> None:
    ...
    self._status: dict[str, DocumentStatus] = {}

def save_document(self, doc_id, text, title=None, status=DocumentStatus.FINALIZED) -> None:
    ...  # existing body
    self._status[doc_id] = status

def get_document_status(self, doc_id: str) -> DocumentStatus | None:
    return self._status.get(doc_id)

def finalize_document(self, doc_id: str) -> None:
    if doc_id in self._status:
        self._status[doc_id] = DocumentStatus.FINALIZED

def delete_concept(self, concept_id: str) -> None:
    doc_id = concept_id.split(":", 1)[0]  # existing id convention: {doc_id}:{slug}
    graph = self._graphs.get(doc_id)
    if graph is None:
        return
    graph.concepts = [c for c in graph.concepts if c.id != concept_id]
    self._questions.pop(concept_id, None)
```
`delete_document` also needs `self._status.pop(doc_id, None)` added to its existing cleanup list.

**`PostgresStore`:** add a `status` column to `DocumentRow` (`backend/app/db/models.py`):
```python
status: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(server_default=DocumentStatus.FINALIZED.value)
```
`server_default` backfills every pre-existing row as `finalized` — correct, since they all went through the full pipeline before this feature existed. One new Alembic migration (`alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add document status"`), additive only, no data migration needed.

```python
def save_document(self, doc_id, text, title=None, status=DocumentStatus.FINALIZED) -> None:
    stmt = pg_insert(DocumentRow).values(id=doc_id, text=text, title=title, status=status.value)
    stmt = stmt.on_conflict_do_update(
        index_elements=["id"],
        set_={"text": stmt.excluded.text, "title": stmt.excluded.title, "status": stmt.excluded.status},
    )
    session.execute(stmt)

def get_document_status(self, doc_id: str) -> DocumentStatus | None:
    with session_scope() as session:
        row = session.get(DocumentRow, doc_id)
        return DocumentStatus(row.status) if row else None

def finalize_document(self, doc_id: str) -> None:
    with session_scope() as session:
        session.execute(
            update(DocumentRow).where(DocumentRow.id == doc_id).values(status=DocumentStatus.FINALIZED.value)
        )

def delete_concept(self, concept_id: str) -> None:
    """Cascades to that concept's own questions via the existing ON DELETE CASCADE
    relationship — moot in practice, since a concept is only ever deleted pre-finalize,
    before it has any questions."""
    with session_scope() as session:
        session.execute(delete(ConceptRow).where(ConceptRow.id == concept_id))
```

`list_documents()` (both backends) needs one more filter clause: **drafts must not appear in the "your chapters" list.** Today it excludes zero-concept documents; a draft *has* concepts (extraction already ran) but hasn't been reviewed, so add `status == FINALIZED` alongside the existing `total_concepts > 0` check. Without this, a draft chapter would show up as openable, and clicking it would land on the ordinary graph view with a "Start tutoring session" button that 409s (see §4).

## 4. Backend — ingestion pipeline

`routers/ingestion.py` currently does `build_graph → generate_questions → save_graph → save_questions*` inline, with a rollback-on-exception wrapping all of it. Split the tail into a shared helper so the "off" path and the new "finalize" path can't drift:

```python
def _finalize(store: Store, graph: DependencyGraph) -> None:
    """Generate questions for every concept and persist the finished graph. Shared by
    the auto-finalize path (edit mode off) and the explicit POST .../finalize endpoint
    (edit mode on) so they can't diverge."""
    question_generator.generate_questions(graph)
    store.save_graph(graph)
    for concept in graph.concepts:
        store.save_questions(concept.id, concept.questions)


@router.post("/textbook", response_model=TextbookUploadResponse, status_code=201)
def upload_textbook(payload: TextbookUpload) -> TextbookUploadResponse:
    if not payload.text.strip():
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="Chapter text must not be blank")

    store = get_store()
    doc_id = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]
    status = DocumentStatus.DRAFT if payload.review else DocumentStatus.FINALIZED
    store.save_document(doc_id, payload.text, payload.title, status=status)

    try:
        graph = graph_builder.build_graph(doc_id, payload.text)
        if not graph.concepts:
            raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="...")  # unchanged
        if payload.review:
            # Pause here for review — persist the as-extracted graph and stop short of
            # question generation. POST /api/textbook/{doc_id}/finalize resumes this.
            store.save_graph(graph)
        else:
            _finalize(store, graph)
    except Exception:
        store.delete_document(doc_id)
        raise

    return TextbookUploadResponse(doc_id=doc_id, status=status)
```

`TextbookUploadResponse` gains `status: DocumentStatus` — the frontend needs it immediately after upload to decide whether to route to the graph view or the new review page, with no extra round trip for the common (mode-off) case.

Two new endpoints on the same router:

```python
@router.get("/{doc_id}/status", response_model=DocumentStatusResponse)
def get_document_status(doc_id: str) -> DocumentStatusResponse:
    status = get_store().get_document_status(doc_id)
    if status is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=f"No document '{doc_id}'")
    return DocumentStatusResponse(status=status)


@router.post("/{doc_id}/finalize", response_model=TextbookUploadResponse)
def finalize_textbook(doc_id: str) -> TextbookUploadResponse:
    store = get_store()
    current = store.get_document_status(doc_id)
    if current is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=f"No document '{doc_id}'")
    if current == DocumentStatus.FINALIZED:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=409, detail="Document is already finalized")
    graph = store.get_graph(doc_id)
    if graph is None or not graph.concepts:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="Cannot finalize a graph with no concepts")
    _finalize(store, graph)
    store.finalize_document(doc_id)
    return TextbookUploadResponse(doc_id=doc_id, status=DocumentStatus.FINALIZED)
```

`GET .../status` is the only way to observe a draft's state at all — `list_documents()` hides drafts and `GET /api/graph/{id}` doesn't say which side of finalize a chapter is on. `POST .../finalize` mirrors the empty-graph rejection `upload_textbook` already has, and 409s on a chapter that is already finalized (whether by approval or by never having asked for review).

`study_session.py`'s `start_study_session` needs one new guard, right after the existing graph-not-found check:
```python
if store.get_document_status(payload.doc_id) == DocumentStatus.DRAFT:
    raise HTTPException(status_code=409, detail="Document is still in review; finalize its graph first")
```
This is unconditional (no flag check needed) — when the mode is off, no document is ever `DRAFT`, so the branch is simply dead code in that deployment.

## 5. Backend — graph mutation endpoints

New router, `backend/app/routers/graph_edit.py`, registered in `main.py` alongside the others. One shared dependency does every guard these endpoints need — the document exists, is still a draft, and has a graph:

```python
def _draft_graph(doc_id: str) -> tuple[Store, DependencyGraph]:
    store = get_store()
    status = store.get_document_status(doc_id)
    if status is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=f"No document '{doc_id}'")
    if status is not DocumentStatus.DRAFT:
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=409,
            detail="Chapter is finalized; its graph is locked. Upload with review enabled "
            "to edit a graph before its questions are written.",
        )
    graph = store.get_graph(doc_id)
    if graph is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=f"No graph found for doc '{doc_id}'")
    return store, graph
```

The 409's message names the fix because the likeliest way to reach it is a forgotten checkbox, not a client bug.

```python
class NewConceptPayload(BaseModel):
    slug: str = Field(pattern=r"^[a-z0-9_]+$")
    name: str
    summary: str

class ConceptEditPayload(BaseModel):
    name: str | None = None
    summary: str | None = None

class EdgePayload(BaseModel):
    from_: str = Field(alias="from")
    to: str


@router.post("/{doc_id}/concepts", response_model=Concept, status_code=201)
def add_concept(doc_id: str, payload: NewConceptPayload, dep=Depends(_draft_graph)) -> Concept:
    store, graph = dep
    concept_id = f"{doc_id}:{payload.slug}"
    if any(c.id == concept_id for c in graph.concepts):
        raise HTTPException(status_code=409, detail="Concept id already exists")
    concept = Concept(id=concept_id, name=payload.name, summary=payload.summary)
    graph.concepts.append(concept)
    store.save_graph(graph)
    return concept


@router.patch("/{doc_id}/concepts/{concept_id}", response_model=Concept)
def edit_concept(doc_id: str, concept_id: str, payload: ConceptEditPayload, dep=Depends(_draft_graph)) -> Concept:
    store, graph = dep
    concept = next((c for c in graph.concepts if c.id == concept_id), None)
    if concept is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=f"Unknown concept '{concept_id}'")
    if payload.name is not None:
        concept.name = payload.name
    if payload.summary is not None:
        concept.summary = payload.summary
    store.save_graph(graph)
    return concept


@router.delete("/{doc_id}/concepts/{concept_id}", status_code=204)
def delete_concept(doc_id: str, concept_id: str, dep=Depends(_draft_graph)) -> None:
    store, graph = dep
    if not any(c.id == concept_id for c in graph.concepts):
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=f"Unknown concept '{concept_id}'")
    remaining = [
        Concept(
            id=c.id, name=c.name, summary=c.summary,
            depends_on=[d for d in c.depends_on if d != concept_id],
            evidence={k: v for k, v in c.evidence.items() if k != concept_id},
        )
        for c in graph.concepts if c.id != concept_id
    ]
    store.save_graph(DependencyGraph(doc_id=doc_id, concepts=remaining))
    store.delete_concept(concept_id)


@router.post("/{doc_id}/edges", status_code=204)
def add_edge(doc_id: str, payload: EdgePayload, dep=Depends(_draft_graph)) -> None:
    store, graph = dep
    by_id = {c.id: c for c in graph.concepts}
    if payload.from_ not in by_id or payload.to not in by_id:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Unknown concept id")
    if payload.from_ == payload.to:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="A concept cannot depend on itself")
    if graph_builder.creates_cycle(graph, payload.from_, payload.to):
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="That edge would introduce a cycle")
    target = by_id[payload.to]
    if payload.from_ not in target.depends_on:
        target.depends_on.append(payload.from_)
        # No source-text quote for a manually added edge. question_generator.source_passages()
        # already skips any evidence entry that's falsy (see add() there), so this doesn't
        # need any change on the question-generation side — it just means this edge won't
        # contribute a `prerequisite_link` passage, only the prerequisite's own summary.
        target.evidence[payload.from_] = ""
    store.save_graph(graph)


@router.delete("/{doc_id}/edges", status_code=204)
def delete_edge(doc_id: str, payload: EdgePayload, dep=Depends(_draft_graph)) -> None:
    store, graph = dep
    by_id = {c.id: c for c in graph.concepts}
    target = by_id.get(payload.to)
    if target is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Unknown concept id")
    target.depends_on = [d for d in target.depends_on if d != payload.from_]
    target.evidence.pop(payload.from_, None)
    store.save_graph(graph)
```

Everything here goes through the existing `save_graph()` upsert-per-concept contract (docs/specs/2026-08-21-persistent-storage-design.md §3) — add/edit-concept and add/delete-edge are all just "upsert a concept row with a different `depends_on`/`evidence`/`name`/`summary`," so no new store method is needed for them. Only whole-concept **deletion** needed a new method, because `save_graph` deliberately never deletes a row missing from its list (same upsert-only reasoning as `save_questions`, see the Storage bullet in CLAUDE.md) — so removing a `ConceptRow` for real requires its own call.

## 6. `graph_builder.py`: a shared cycle check

The edge-add endpoint needs the exact same cycle-safety rule `build_graph()` already applies to LLM-extracted edges — a manually-added edge must be rejected on the same terms an auto-extracted one would be. Generalize the existing `_reachable` instead of writing a second, possibly-divergent check:

```python
def _reachable(neighbors: Callable[[str], Iterable[str]], start: str, target: str) -> bool:
    stack = [start]
    seen: set[str] = set()
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if node == target:
            return True
        if node in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(node)
        stack.extend(neighbors(node))
    return False


def creates_cycle(graph: DependencyGraph, frm: str, to: str) -> bool:
    """Whether adding an edge where `to` depends on `frm` would introduce a cycle —
    i.e. whether `frm` already (transitively) depends on `to`. Public so the graph-edit
    endpoints apply exactly the rule build_graph() applies to LLM-extracted edges."""
    by_id = {c.id: c for c in graph.concepts}
    return _reachable(lambda n: by_id[n].depends_on if n in by_id else [], frm, to)
```

`build_graph()`'s own loop switches from its private dict-shaped check to `_reachable(lambda n: deps.get(n, {}), frm, to)` — same function, two adjacency shapes (raw extraction's `dict[str, dict[str, str]]` vs. the public `Concept.depends_on` lists), no behavior change to existing extraction.

