**`MenuPage.tsx`** carries the opt-in: a `<label className="upload-option">` under the textarea wrapping a checkbox and its explanation (one click target), with `review` state defaulting to `false`. The submit button relabels to "Extract concepts" when it's ticked, since that is then all the request does.

**A fourth page**, `ReviewGraphPage.tsx` — not a mode bolted onto `GraphView.tsx`. `GraphView` is a read-only viewer plus a "start session" CTA; review needs edit forms and *no* start-session affordance at all (blocked server-side anyway per §4), so the two have little in common beyond both fetching a `DependencyGraph`. `App.tsx`'s own comment already anticipated this exact moment — its plain-state router was deliberately kept simple "with three pages," noting "revisit when a fourth page appears." This is that fourth page.

`ReviewGraphPage` renders `DependencyGraphViz` read-only (unchanged component, no drag/rewire — there's no graph-layout library in `frontend/package.json` today and adding one is out of scope, see §8) alongside a form-based editor below it, reusing the app's existing interaction idioms rather than inventing new ones:
- Each concept row: name/summary shown as text, an "Edit" button that swaps the row into two inputs + Save/Cancel (same swap-in-place idiom `SessionList.tsx` already uses for delete confirmation), and a "Delete" button using that same inline-confirm pattern.
- An "Add concept" form: slug/name/summary inputs + submit.
- Per concept, its current prerequisites listed with a small remove ("×") control each, plus a `<select>` of the graph's other concepts to add a new one.
- A "Finalize & generate questions" button, disabled when there are zero concepts, calling `finalizeGraph(docId)` and then `onFinalized(docId)` on success.
- Errors surfaced the same way as every other page: a `<p className="error">` above the relevant section, pessimistic updates (state changes only after the request succeeds), no toasts/undo — matching the minimalism the delete-study-session design settled on.

**`App.tsx`:** add `'review'` to the `Page` union. `onOpenDocument` needs the doc's status to decide where to go, so its signature grows a second, optional argument:
