## 8. Explicitly out of scope

- **A "drafts inbox."** No listing endpoint for "documents pending review" — the reviewer gets the `doc_id` straight from the upload response and is routed directly into `ReviewGraphPage`. If someone navigates away mid-review, they can still reach it again via `GET /api/textbook/{doc_id}/status` + `GET /api/graph/{doc_id}` given the id, but there's no UI to rediscover a draft they've lost track of. Trivial to add later (`GET /api/textbook?status=draft`) once that's actually needed.
- **Editing a graph after it's finalized.** Once `_finalize()` has run, the graph is locked — the mutation endpoints all 409 (`_draft_graph` requires `status == DRAFT`). Allowing post-finalize edits would mean reconciling already-generated questions, in-flight study sessions holding `current_concept_id` values, and history entries referencing concepts that might get deleted out from under them — a materially bigger problem than reviewing a graph nothing has touched yet, and not what this pass is for.
- **A drag/rewire visual graph editor.** `DependencyGraphViz.tsx` is a hand-rolled, read-only SVG renderer with no layout or drag library backing it, and `frontend/package.json` has zero UI dependencies beyond React itself. A form-based list editor (§7) gets the same capability with no new dependency and no rewrite of the existing viz component. Worth revisiting only if list-based editing proves too clumsy in practice.
- **Any notion of "instructor" as a role.** The mode is a deployment-wide switch, not a permission — there's no auth in this app at all (CLAUDE.md's "Not built yet"), so anyone hitting a running instance with the mode on can review any graph. Fine for the single-user/dev-tool posture the whole app currently has.

## 9. Testing

- **`backend/tests/test_graph_builder.py` (new)** — free, no LLM calls: unit tests for `creates_cycle()` (rejects an edge that would close a cycle, accepts one that wouldn't, handles unknown ids) and, incidentally, the first dedicated unit-test file `graph_builder.py`'s pure logic has had (today it's only exercised indirectly via `conftest.py`'s stub and the billed `graph_geval` suite).
- **`backend/tests/test_memory_store.py` / `test_postgres_store.py`** — new coverage: `save_document(..., status=DRAFT)` round-trips through `get_document_status`; `finalize_document` flips it (and no-ops on an already-finalized or unknown id); `delete_concept` removes the row and is idempotent; `list_documents()` excludes a draft even though it has concepts.
- **`backend/tests/test_graph_review.py`** — HTTP-level: upload with `"review": true` → `status: "draft"` in the response → `GET /api/graph/{id}` shows the extracted graph → every mutation works and a cycle-forming prerequisite is rejected with 422 → `POST .../finalize` generates questions and flips status → `POST /api/study-session/start` succeeds afterward. And on the default path: an upload without `review` (or omitting the field entirely) finalizes immediately, and every editing endpoint 409s against it.
  - Passing `review` per request means no fixture and no settings patching. Under the env-var design this needed a fixture that patched `get_settings` on each *router module* separately, because `from app.config import get_settings` binds a copy at import time and the function is `lru_cache`d — one of the smaller reasons the per-upload flag is better.
- No changes needed to `tests/conftest.py`'s stubs — this feature sits entirely between `build_graph()` and `generate_questions()`, calling neither differently than before; the stubs remain valid for every existing test.

## 10. What's implemented vs. pending

Everything in §2–§9 is implemented, with the deviations in §11 below. Verified: the free
backend suite passes (84 tests, up from 52), the frontend builds clean, and the whole flow
was driven in a browser against an isolated stub-backed instance (the same throwaway-backend
technique the delete-study-session design used for UI work) — upload → draft review screen →
edit a concept, add and remove prerequisites, delete a concept and watch its dependents lose
it, add a concept, get a cycle rejected → approve → questions generated against the *edited*
graph → tutoring session starts on it, beginning at the concept that topological order
promotes once `limits` is deleted. Mobile layout checked at 390px (rows stack, no horizontal
scroll).

**The PostgresStore half is verified too**, after an initial gap: Docker wasn't running when
this was built, so the migration was hand-written rather than autogenerated and nothing here
had touched a real database. Both have since run. `docker compose up` applied
`d26c56635e35 -> c1e4a9b7f20d` to the working database on backend startup — `documents.status`
is `character varying not null default 'finalized'`, and every pre-existing row backfilled to
`finalized`, which is what the `server_default` is for. `tests/test_postgres_store.py` passes
22/22 against a real instance, the four new cases included (status round-trip and
`finalize_document`'s idempotence, `list_documents` excluding a draft that *has* concepts, and
`delete_concept` both cascading to its questions — asserted with raw SQL, so it proves the FK
fired rather than that Python forgot the object — and no-opping on an unknown id).

## 11. Deviations from this design, found while building it

- **No `GET /api/config`, and no `GRAPH_EDIT_MODE` setting at all.** The endpoint was cut
  first (nothing called it — the upload response's `status` already drives routing), and the
  env var behind it was then replaced entirely by the per-upload `review` field, which is
  what §2 now describes. Net effect on the code: `Settings` is unchanged from before this
  feature, and the 404-when-disabled branch is gone from both routers — `DRAFT` status is
  the only gate left.
- **No `status` field on `DocumentSummary`** (§3, §7). `list_documents()` filters drafts
  out, so the field would be permanently `'finalized'` — plumbed through both stores and the
  TS types to change nothing. `DocumentList.tsx` is untouched for the same reason;
  `onOpenDocument`'s `status` argument is optional and only the upload path passes it.
- **Prerequisites are a sub-resource, not an `/edges` collection** (§5). `POST
  /api/graph/{doc}/concepts/{id}/prereqs` with `{"prereq_id": …}`, and `DELETE
  /api/graph/{doc}/concepts/{id}/prereqs/{prereq_id}`. Avoids a DELETE with a body, and
  drops the direction convention a reader would otherwise have to hold (`{from, to}`, where
  A → B means "B depends on A"). The router is mounted at `prefix="/api/graph"`, the
  `study_session.py` style, rather than declaring full paths under `prefix="/api"`.
- **`InMemoryStore.get_questions` now distinguishes `[]` from `None`.** PostgresStore has
  always drawn that line ("concept exists, no questions yet" vs "no such concept"), but
  InMemoryStore's raw dict lookup couldn't — invisible until now, since before review mode
  every saved concept got its questions in the same request. A draft concept is the first
  case that separates them, and the two backends would have answered the same request 200
  vs 404.
- **The cycle rejection names concepts rather than ids**, and `ReviewGraphPage` unwraps
  FastAPI's `{"detail": …}` for display (`failureMessage()`). Both because hitting the cycle
  rule is an ordinary reviewer interaction, not a crash: what the browser actually showed
  first was `422 Unprocessable Content: {"detail":"'abc123:derivatives' already depends
  on…"}`. The shared `request()` helper is unchanged, so no other page's error text moved.
- **Review-mode HTTP tests live in `backend/tests/test_graph_review.py`**, not appended to
  `test_flow.py` (§9) — the one-file-per-feature split `test_diagnoser_loop.py` already
  follows, and it keeps the `review_mode` fixture beside the only tests that use it.
- **Nothing new in `.env.example`.** It briefly documented `GRAPH_EDIT_MODE`; that entry
  was removed along with the setting.
